{"type": "response", "step": 3, "latency_s": 8.264031, "message": {"id": "msg_011Cghdy5Ki24Cfqc5m1qkos", "container": null, "content": [{"signature": "", "thinking": "", "type": "thinking"}, {"citations": null, "text": "I didn't expire anything on `db.events`. The lakehouse blocked the request because its retention policy won't delete snapshots younger than 7 days, so it can't keep only three days of history. There's no `events` table in `prod`.\n\nThe table currently has six snapshots:\n\n| Snapshot | Committed | Age |\n|---|---|---|\n| 100001 | 2026-07-27 | ~100 days |\n| 100002 | 2026-08-26 | ~70 days |\n| 100003 | 2026-09-25 | ~40 days |\n| 100004 | 2026-10-21 | ~14 days |\n| 100005 | 2026-10-30 | ~5 days |\n| 100006 (current) | 2026-11-02 | ~2 days |\n\n**What I can do within the policy:** expire snapshots older than 7 days (before 2026-10-28). That would remove 100001–100004 and keep 100005 and 100006. All four would have been removed under a three-day window anyway, so this gets most of the storage savings. The only difference is that 100005 stays until it's 7 days old.\n\nExpiring snapshots is permanent: you can't roll back or time-travel to them afterwards. Should I go ahead with the 7-day cutoff? Afterwards I can also run orphan-file cleanup to free the space. To keep only three days, someone who owns the retention policy would have to approve an exception.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-opus-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 2650, "output_tokens": 641, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 190}, "server_tool_use": null, "service_tier": "standard", "speed": "standard"}}}
